    "README.md": "# Bakery Bern\n\nA one-page website for a small bakery in Bern.\n\n## Files\n\n- `index.html`: the whole page (name, menu, hours, address)\n- `style.css`: styling\n\n## Run\n\nOpen `index.html` in a browser. There is no build step and nothing to install.\n\n## Real content\n\nThe owner will supply the real menu, opening hours and address. Until then every shop fact on the page is marked `PLACEHOLDER`. Find them with:\n\n```\ngrep -n PLACEHOLDER index.html\n```\n\nReplace each one with the real value and remove the marker.\n",
    "docs/decisions/0001-static-html-page.md": "# 0001: Plain static HTML and CSS\n\nDecision: Build the site as a single static `index.html` with `style.css`, no framework, no build step, no JavaScript, opened locally for now.\n\n## Why\n\nThe brief asks for one page with a menu, hours and an address. Plain files are the simplest thing that does this. They are easy for the owner to edit, load fast, and can later be hosted anywhere (any static host) without changes. No hosting or domain has been chosen; that is a separate decision for when the site is ready to publish.\n\n## Content\n\nThe real menu, hours and address are not available yet. The page is built with placeholders, each marked `PLACEHOLDER`, so nothing invented can be mistaken for a real fact. Real content replaces them when supplied.\n\n## Language\n\nThe page is set to `lang=\"de-CH\"`, as Bern is German-speaking. This is a one-attribute change if the owner wants another language or several.\n\n## Constraints\n\nNo external requests (fonts, scripts, map embeds), so the page works offline and has no tracking or cookie concerns. The address links to a map search instead of embedding a map.\n"
  },
